Uncomplicated viral pharyngitis with mild subjective throat discomfort and normal swallowing
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Pharyngeal diffuse erythema, Absence of tonsillar exudate, Preserved oral intake

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-050
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of sore throat requiring assessment to rule out severe bacterial tonsillitis, peritonsillar abscess, or impending airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden throat pain. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to perform an oropharyngeal exam, definitively rule out a peritonsillar abscess (quinsy) which can obstruct the airway, and ensure the patient was maintaining adequate oral hydration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My throat is very scratchy and dry, and it hurts slightly when I swallow.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a mild generalized scratchy or dry feeling in the posterior pharynx developing over the last 24 hours. Reports tolerable odynophagia that is not impairing oral fluid intake. Accompanying mild viral coryzal symptoms (sneezing, clear runny nose) are present. Complete absence of high-grade fever, severe unilateral throat pain, or difficulty opening the mouth (trismus).
Functional Status: Fully independent, eating and drinking normally.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fear of transmitting the infection to vulnerable household contacts.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 80 | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, speaking with normal vocal quality (no "hot potato" voice).
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals mild diffuse erythema of the tonsils and posterior pharyngeal wall. Diagnostic triad confirmed: pharyngeal diffuse erythema, absence of thick white tonsillar exudates, and preserved oral intake. Normal uvular position without significant edema or deviation. Lack of tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Normal neck range of motion without torticollis.
Environmental Observations: Water bottle present, patient sipping comfortably.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient trialing throat lozenges with good effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncomplicated viral pharyngitis with mild subjective throat discomfort and normal swallowing - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Streptococcal Pharyngitis (Strep Throat) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, lack of tonsillar exudates, absence of tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and presence of viral coryzal symptoms (cough/runny nose).

Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, midline uvula, absence of trismus, and symmetric mild tonsillar erythema without severe unilateral swelling.

Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal swallowing, clear voice, no stridor, and absence of drooling or toxic appearance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute sore throat mandates a direct physical examination to exclude critical airway threats like epiglottitis or peritonsillar abscess, which require immediate emergency intervention. Delaying the assessment carried a risk of airway compromise if a deep space infection was evolving. The urgent home evaluation confidently confirmed a symmetric, mild viral erythema with a fully patent airway and normal swallowing, safely avoiding unnecessary antibiotic prescribing and ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance that antibiotics are not indicated for viral infections. Recommendation for regular paracetamol or ibuprofen for pain relief, and anesthetic throat lozenges.
Positive Behavioral Change: Gargle with warm salt water (1/2 teaspoon salt in a glass of warm water) before every meal to physically wash away loose mucus and soothe the inflamed mucosa, making eating more comfortable.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the sore throat persists for more than 7 days or worsens significantly.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you suddenly become unable to swallow your own saliva, develop noisy breathing (stridor), or notice swelling on the outside of your neck.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical viral syndrome; a rapid strep test (if done) would be expected to be negative.
Clinical Course: The scratchiness will typically peak at days 2-3 and resolve entirely within 5 to 7 days as the virus runs its course.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you become unable to open your mouth fully, if the pain becomes incredibly severe on one side only, or if you develop a high fever with a rash.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A cold virus has inflamed the lining of your throat. Antibiotics only kill bacteria, not viruses, so they will not help and may cause side effects. Your immune system is effectively handling the virus.
Actionable Steps: Stay well hydrated with cool or warm liquids. Use throat lozenges and regular pain relief. Do not ask for antibiotics.

